2.1 First identity. By [F1] and the linearity of the divergence [F4], $\operatorname{div}E=\operatorname{div}(-
abla\varphi-\partial_tA)=-\operatorname{div}
abla\varphi-\operatorname{div}\partial_tA$. The definition of the Laplacian [F6] gives $\operatorname{div}
abla\varphi=\Delta\varphi$, and the componentwise commutation of the time and space partial derivatives of the $C^2$ field $A$ [F5] gives $\operatorname{div}\partial_tA=\partial_t\operatorname{div}A$. On the other hand, expanding $G$ [F1] and using the linearity of $\partial_t$ and of $\Delta$ [F4] with [F5], $W\varphi-\partial_tG=(c^{-2}\partial_t^2\varphi-\Delta\varphi)-(\partial_t\operatorname{div}A+c^{-2}\partial_t^2\varphi)=-\Delta\varphi-\partial_t\operatorname{div}A$. The two right-hand sides agree, so $\operatorname{div}E=W\varphi-\partial_tG$ on $\Omega	imes I$. [step 1.1, F1, F4, F5, F6]

2.4 Gauge shift and the Lorenz condition. Let $\chi$ be a gauge function of class $C^2$ jointly. By [F1] the transform $T_\chi(\varphi,A)=(\varphi-\partial_t\chi,\ A+
abla\chi)$ is a potential pair of class $C^1$, so its Lorenz divergence is defined; expanding it with the linearity of $\operatorname{div}$ [F4] and the definitions [F1], $G(T_\chi(\varphi,A))=\operatorname{div}A+\operatorname{div}
abla\chi+c^{-2}\partial_t\varphi-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\chi=G+\Delta\chi-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\chi=G-W\chi$, the middle identity using $\operatorname{div}
abla\chi=\Delta\chi$ [F6] and the last the definition of $W$ [F1]. Thus $G(T_\chi(\varphi,A))=0$ if and only if $W\chi=G$ on $\Omega	imes I$; and the field pair is unchanged by [F9], so the transformed pair represents the same $(E,B)$ of [F3]. [step 1.1, F1, F4, F6, F9]

